In the 1950s approximately 30% of the population of Italy was involved in agriculture. The system of mezzadria was abolished in the 1960s as part of post-war agricultural reform, but since the war farmers had been drifting away from the land to the city for more regulated, better paid jobs and a more comfortable, secure life.
